/// \defgroup grp_w1012_wwlib_mpmath_arith WWLib multi-precision arithmetic routines
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief The XMP_ routines in mpmath.cpp that add, subtract, compare,
///        shift, negate, multiply and divide raw digit arrays, and the
///        helpers behind XMP_Mod_Mult.
///
/// These routines are the arithmetic layer under the Int big-integer class
/// and the PKey code in \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath. That group also
/// describes the number format (32-bit digits, least significant first, two's
/// complement), the file as a whole, and the exponentiation, division and
/// primality routines built on these. The entries listed here are on the
/// \ref mpmath.cpp "mpmath.cpp" file page:
/// - add and subtract with a carry or borrow: XMP_Add, XMP_Add_Int,
///   XMP_Sub and XMP_Sub_Int;
/// - compare, measure and negate: XMP_Compare, XMP_Count_Bits and XMP_Neg;
/// - shift and rotate: XMP_Shift_Left_Bits, XMP_Shift_Right_Bits and
///   XMP_Rotate_Left;
/// - multiply and divide by a 16-bit value: XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int,
///   XMP_Signed_Mult_Int and XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int;
/// - signed full multiply: XMP_Signed_Mult, the signed front end of
///   XMP_Unsigned_Mult;
/// - the internals of XMP_Mod_Mult: XMP_Double_Mul, XMP_Hybrid_Mul,
///   mp_quo_digit and XMP_Mod_Mult_Clear.
///
/// \par Conventions
/// - The digit count comes last and is usually called precision.
///   XMP_Hybrid_Mul counts 16-bit units instead.
/// - Results wrap. Sums, differences and products keep only precision
///   digits (XMP_Double_Mul is the exception: it returns the full
///   double-length product, and XMP_Hybrid_Mul adds its final carry into
///   the unit above its range). The add and subtract routines return the
///   carry or borrow; the multiply routines report no overflow.
/// - XMP_Compare, XMP_Count_Bits and the Unsigned_ routines read every bit
///   as part of an unsigned value. The signed multiply and divide routines
///   (XMP_Signed_Mult_Int, XMP_Signed_Mult and XMP_Signed_Div) handle
///   signs: they take the magnitudes of multi-digit operands with XMP_Neg
///   (XMP_Signed_Mult_Int negates its 16-bit multiplier as a signed short),
///   work on those unsigned, and fix the sign of the result afterwards.
/// - XMP_Sub, XMP_Sub_Int, XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int and the XMP_Mod_Mult
///   helpers treat each digit as two 16-bit units, low half first, which
///   assumes little-endian storage.
/// - Input ranges that can give wrong results in the shipped code, though
///   not for every input, include shift counts that are a multiple of 32
///   above 32, multipliers of 32768 or more in XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int, a
///   multiplier of -32768 in XMP_Signed_Mult_Int, and divisors above 32768
///   in XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int. The entries for those routines explain why.
/// - Caller lists are source-level. They name the routines in mpmath.cpp,
///   int.h and pk.cpp whose source reaches the entry, directly or through
///   one of Int's operators. A call scan of the retail image finds fewer
///   callers: retail compiles some of those uses inline, and Int's
///   operators are members of a class template, so one that no BFME 2 code
///   uses is never instantiated and has no retail copy. Some entries, such
///   as those for XMP_Compare, XMP_Sub, XMP_Shift_Left_Bits and XMP_Neg,
///   also say which callers reach the out-of-line retail copy.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing here is data-driven, and the game itself does not appear to use
/// this code; \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath gives the evidence and its
/// limits.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Working in 16-bit units keeps every intermediate product and carry
/// within a 32-bit unsigned long. The modular multiplication path,
/// XMP_Double_Mul, XMP_Hybrid_Mul, mp_quo_digit, XMP_Prepare_Modulus and
/// XMP_Mod_Mult, builds on that 16-bit unit size throughout. XMP_Mod_Mult
/// also hands XMP_Sub a digit pointer that can sit at an odd 16-bit offset
/// in its staging buffer. XMP_Sub reads it in 16-bit units, so every access
/// stays aligned; a version of XMP_Sub that stepped in whole digits would
/// read misaligned 32-bit digits there, which x86 tolerates but C++ does
/// not guarantee. A port that widens the units, or rewrites XMP_Sub to
/// work in whole digits, has to change these together.

/// \fn int XMP_Compare(const digit *left_number, const digit *right_number, int precision)
/// \brief Compares two numbers of the same digit count as unsigned values.
///
/// Scans from the most significant digit down and stops at the first digit
/// that differs. Digits are compared as unsigned values and the sign bit
/// gets no special treatment, so a negative number compares greater than
/// every non-negative one. int.h builds Int's ordering operators (>, >=, <
/// and <=) directly on it, so they compare the same way; Generate_Prime
/// uses it through the > operator. In the source of mpmath.cpp it is called
/// by XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Reciprocal, XMP_Randomize_Bounded,
/// XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test and the range checks at the start of
/// XMP_Exponent_Mod. In retail only XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Exponent_Mod,
/// Int's > operator and Generate_Prime call the out-of-line copy; the
/// comparisons in XMP_Reciprocal, XMP_Randomize_Bounded and
/// XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test are compiled inline. No BFME 2 code uses Int's >=,
/// < or <= operators, so those template members are never instantiated.
///
/// \param left_number  The left-hand number.
/// \param right_number The right-hand number.
/// \param precision    Digit count of both. At least 1: the count is
///                     tested only after the first digit is compared.
/// \return -1 if left_number is smaller, 1 if it is larger, 0 if the two
///         are equal.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Exponent_Mod

/// \fn bool XMP_Add(digit *result, const digit *left_number, const digit *right_number, bool carry, int precision)
/// \brief Adds two numbers, with a carry in and a carry out.
///
/// Computes left_number + right_number + carry one digit at a time from the
/// low end. The stored sum keeps only precision digits; the carry out of
/// the top digit is returned instead. Two's-complement addition is the same
/// operation for signed values, so it serves both. Each digit's carry is
/// found by comparison (a sum that wrapped is smaller than its left
/// operand), so no type wider than a digit is needed.
///
/// int.h builds Int's + and += operators on it; they keep the returned
/// carry in Int's static Carry member. In the source, its other callers are
/// XMP_Unsigned_Mult (one call per set multiplier bit), XMP_Signed_Div,
/// XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B, XMP_Randomize_Bounded and Generate_Prime.
///
/// \param result       [out] The sum. May be the same buffer as either
///                     operand: each digit is read before its result is
///                     stored.
/// \param left_number  First operand.
/// \param right_number Second operand.
/// \param carry        Adds one more to the sum when true. Callers in this
///                     library pass false.
/// \param precision    Digit count of all three.
/// \return true if the sum overflowed the top digit.
/// \see XMP_Add_Int, XMP_Sub

/// \fn bool XMP_Add_Int(digit *result, const digit *left_number, digit right_number, bool carry, int precision)
/// \brief Adds a single unsigned digit to a number, with a carry in and a
///        carry out.
///
/// right_number is added to the lowest digit and the carry then ripples
/// through the rest. The loop always runs the full precision; it does not
/// stop once the carry is spent. In the source, its callers are
/// XMP_Decode_ASCII, which adds each parsed character's value after
/// multiplying by the radix, and Int's + operator that takes an unsigned
/// short, which Generate_Prime uses.
///
/// \param result       [out] The sum. May be the same buffer as
///                     left_number.
/// \param left_number  The number added to.
/// \param right_number The value added, as an unsigned 32-bit digit.
/// \param carry        Adds one more to the sum when true.
/// \param precision    Digit count of result and left_number.
/// \return true if the sum overflowed the top digit.
/// \see XMP_Add, XMP_Sub_Int

/// \fn bool XMP_Sub(digit *result, const digit *left_number, const digit *right_number, bool borrow, int precision)
/// \brief Subtracts one number from another, with a borrow in and a borrow
///        out.
///
/// Computes left_number - right_number - borrow. Unlike XMP_Add it works
/// in 16-bit units: it reads every digit as two 16-bit halves, subtracts
/// each pair in a 32-bit intermediate, and takes bit 16 of that result as
/// the borrow into the next half. Reading a digit as low half then high
/// half assumes little-endian storage (see the remastering notes in
/// \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath).
///
/// int.h builds Int's -= operator, and the - operator that takes an Int,
/// on it; they keep the returned borrow in Int's static Borrow member. In
/// the source, its other callers are XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Reciprocal,
/// XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B, XMP_Randomize_Bounded and XMP_Mod_Mult. When a
/// trial quotient from mp_quo_digit was too large, XMP_Mod_Mult subtracts
/// the modulus, at the current unit position, from its one's-complement
/// dividend; on the true value that adds one modulus back at that
/// position, which lowers the trial quotient by one. In retail only
/// XMP_Randomize_Bounded, XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Reciprocal and
/// XMP_Mod_Mult call the out-of-line copy; the
/// subtraction in XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B is compiled inline. No BFME 2 code
/// uses Int's -= operator or the - operator that takes an Int, so those
/// template members are never instantiated. (The - operator that takes an
/// unsigned short is built on XMP_Sub_Int instead.)
///
/// \param result       [out] The difference, kept to precision digits. May
///                     be the same buffer as either operand.
/// \param left_number  The number subtracted from.
/// \param right_number The number subtracted.
/// \param borrow       Subtracts one more when true.
/// \param precision    Digit count of all three.
/// \return true if the subtraction borrowed out of the top digit, which
///         for unsigned values means right_number was larger.
/// \see XMP_Sub_Int, XMP_Add

/// \fn bool XMP_Sub_Int(digit *result, const digit *left_number, unsigned short right_number, bool borrow, int precision)
/// \brief Subtracts a 16-bit value from a number, with a borrow in and a
///        borrow out.
///
/// The single-value form of XMP_Sub, with the same 16-bit-unit scheme:
/// right_number is subtracted from the lowest unit and the borrow ripples
/// through every remaining unit. In the source, its callers are Int's -
/// operator that takes an unsigned short, which Generate_Prime and
/// PKey::Generate use, and XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test.
///
/// \param result       [out] The difference. May be the same buffer as
///                     left_number.
/// \param left_number  The number subtracted from.
/// \param right_number The value subtracted, 0 to 65535.
/// \param borrow       Subtracts one more when true.
/// \param precision    Digit count of result and left_number.
/// \return true if the subtraction borrowed out of the top digit.
/// \see XMP_Sub, XMP_Add_Int

/// \fn int XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int(digit *prod, const digit *multiplicand, short multiplier, int precision)
/// \brief Multiplies a number by a small non-negative integer.
///
/// One pass over the number in 16-bit units: each unit is multiplied by
/// multiplier, the carry from the unit below is added, the low 16 bits are
/// stored and the rest carried up. The carry out of the top unit is
/// dropped (the source has the statement that would store it commented
/// out), so the product keeps only precision digits and overflow is not
/// reported. Each unit is read before it is written, so prod may be the
/// same buffer as multiplicand; XMP_Decode_ASCII multiplies its running
/// value by the radix in place this way.
///
/// In the source, its callers are XMP_Decode_ASCII (radix 2, 8, 10 or 16),
/// XMP_Signed_Mult_Int, Int's * operator that takes an unsigned short, and
/// Generate_Prime, which int.h shows doubling a number with that operator.
///
/// \param prod         [out] The product, kept to precision digits.
/// \param multiplicand The number to multiply.
/// \param multiplier   The factor, 0 to 32767. See the warning.
/// \param precision    Digit count of prod and multiplicand.
/// \return Always 0.
/// \warning multiplier is a signed short, and retail widens it with sign
///          extension before multiplying. A value of 32768 or more, which
///          Int's * operator can pass because it takes an unsigned short,
///          arrives as a negative number and the carries can come out
///          wrong. Multiplying 1 by 32768, for example, sets bits 15 to 31
///          rather than bit 15 alone.
///          XMP_Decode_ASCII passes 2 to 16 and Generate_Prime passes 2.
/// \see XMP_Signed_Mult_Int, XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int

/// \fn int XMP_Signed_Mult_Int(digit *prod, const digit *multiplicand, signed short multiplier, int precision)
/// \brief Multiplies a signed number by a signed 16-bit integer.
///
/// Multiplies the magnitudes with XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int and negates the
/// product with XMP_Neg when exactly one input is negative. A negative
/// multiplicand is copied to a local array before it is negated, and a
/// non-negative one is safe to multiply in place, so prod may be the same
/// buffer as multiplicand. The product keeps only precision digits.
///
/// \param prod         [out] The product.
/// \param multiplicand The signed number to multiply.
/// \param multiplier   The signed factor, -32767 to 32767.
/// \param precision    Digit count of prod and multiplicand.
/// \return Always 0.
/// \note A multiplier of -32768 is negated in a signed short, so it stays
///       -32768 and XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int gives a wrong product.
/// \note A scan of the retail image finds no call, jump or stored address
///       that targets this function, and int.h has no operator built on
///       it.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int, XMP_Signed_Mult

/// \fn int XMP_Signed_Mult(digit *prod, const digit *multiplicand, const digit *multiplier, int precision)
/// \brief Multiplies two signed numbers.
///
/// The signed front end of XMP_Unsigned_Mult. Each negative operand is
/// copied to a local array and negated there, the magnitudes are
/// multiplied, and the product is negated with XMP_Neg when the signs
/// differ. The product keeps only precision digits and overflow is not
/// reported.
///
/// int.h builds Int's * and *= operators on it and stores its return
/// value in Int's static Error member. The *= operator first copies the
/// left-hand value into Int's static Remainder member and multiplies from
/// that copy. PKey::Generate also calls it, with Int's multiplication
/// compiled inline.
///
/// \param prod         [out] The product. It must not overlap an operand
///                     that is not negative: XMP_Unsigned_Mult clears prod
///                     before it reads that operand, which is passed to it
///                     unchanged.
/// \param multiplicand First signed factor.
/// \param multiplier   Second signed factor.
/// \param precision    Digit count of all three.
/// \return Always 0. The return value of XMP_Unsigned_Mult, also always 0,
///         is not checked.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Mult, XMP_Signed_Mult_Int, XMP_Signed_Div

/// \fn unsigned short XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int(digit *quotient, digit const *dividend, unsigned short divisor, int precision)
/// \brief Divides a number by a 16-bit divisor and returns the remainder.
///
/// Shift-and-subtract long division, one bit at a time over the
/// dividend's significant bits (XMP_Count_Bits), with a 16-bit running
/// remainder. The dividend is read as unsigned, so a negative value is
/// divided as the large positive number its bits spell.
///
/// In the source, its callers are XMP_Small_Divisors_Test, which divides by
/// every prime in the table and looks for a zero remainder; RemainderTable's
/// constructor, which records the remainder for each table prime; and two
/// Int operators that take an unsigned short from int.h: / returns the
/// quotient and stores the remainder in Int's static Error member, and %
/// passes Int's static Remainder member as the quotient buffer and returns
/// the remainder.
///
/// \param quotient  [out] The quotient, rounded down. It is cleared before
///                  the dividend is read, so it must not overlap dividend.
/// \param dividend  The number divided.
/// \param divisor   The divisor, 1 to 32768. See the warning.
/// \param precision Digit count of quotient and dividend.
/// \return The remainder. A zero divisor returns 0 at once and leaves
///         quotient unwritten.
/// \warning The running remainder is doubled in 16 bits, so its top bit
///          is lost once it reaches 32768. That can only happen with a
///          divisor above 32768, and then the results can be wrong (not
///          for every dividend). The table primes that
///          XMP_Small_Divisors_Test and RemainderTable divide by are at
///          most 32719.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Small_Divisors_Test, RemainderTable

/// \fn void XMP_Double_Mul(digit *prod, const digit *multiplicand, const digit *multiplier, int precision)
/// \brief Forms the full double-length product of two non-negative
///        numbers.
///
/// The first step of XMP_Mod_Mult, its only caller in the source and in
/// retail, which passes its file-scope staging buffer as prod. The product
/// is cleared, then schoolbook multiplication runs in 16-bit units: for each
/// unit of multiplier, XMP_Hybrid_Mul adds multiplicand times that unit into
/// prod, starting one unit higher each time. Nothing is truncated, unlike
/// XMP_Unsigned_Mult, which keeps only precision digits.
///
/// \param prod         [out] Receives 2 * precision digits. Must not
///                     overlap either input.
/// \param multiplicand First factor, read as unsigned.
/// \param multiplier   Second factor, read as unsigned.
/// \param precision    Digit count of each input.
/// \see XMP_Hybrid_Mul, XMP_Mod_Mult, XMP_Unsigned_Mult

/// \fn void XMP_Mod_Mult_Clear(int precision)
/// \brief Wipes the modulus and scratch values that modular multiplication
///        leaves in file-scope statics.
///
/// XMP_Prepare_Modulus and XMP_Mod_Mult keep their state in statics of
/// mpmath.cpp: a copy of the modulus with its bit and 16-bit-unit lengths,
/// the two work arrays used to compute its reciprocal, the reciprocal's two
/// 16-bit halves and shift count, and a double-width staging buffer. This
/// function zeroes them. XMP_Exponent_Mod, its only caller in the source and
/// in retail, calls it at the end of every exponentiation with the modulus's
/// significant digit count. The source's comment there asks it to burn its
/// own evidence, that is, to leave no key-derived values in memory.
///
/// \param precision Number of digits to clear in the modulus copy and in
///                  the staging buffer. Pass the digit count given to
///                  XMP_Prepare_Modulus.
/// \note Only the low precision digits of the staging buffer are cleared.
///       It holds a double-width product, so its upper half is left as it
///       is.
/// \see XMP_Prepare_Modulus, XMP_Mod_Mult, XMP_Exponent_Mod

/// \fn void XMP_Shift_Left_Bits(digit *number, int bits, int precision)
/// \brief Shifts a number left by any number of bits, in place, filling
///        with zeros.
///
/// Bits shifted past the top digit are lost and nothing reports it. A
/// count of zero does nothing. There are three paths: a count of exactly
/// 32 moves whole digits; a count below 32 ripples the top bits of each
/// digit into the next; a larger count moves whole digits and shifts by
/// the remaining bits at the same time.
///
/// In the source, XMP_Unsigned_Mult, XMP_Unsigned_Div and XMP_Reciprocal
/// shift by one bit per loop step with it. int.h builds Int's << and <<=
/// operators on it, and Generate_Prime builds its lower and upper bounds by
/// shifting 1 left. In retail the one-bit shifts are compiled inline, and
/// only Int's << operator and Generate_Prime call the out-of-line copy. No
/// BFME 2 code uses Int's <<= operator, so that template member is never
/// instantiated.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number to shift.
/// \param bits      Shift count, 0 or more.
/// \param precision Digit count of number.
/// \warning Counts that are a multiple of 32 other than 32 itself (64, 96
///          and so on) can give a wrong result. The general path fills
///          each digit with bits taken from the digit below by shifting
///          that digit right by 32 minus the remaining bit count. With no
///          remaining bits that is a shift by 32, which C leaves undefined;
///          the x86 shift instruction retail uses keeps only the low five
///          bits of the count, shifts by 0, and so ORs the lower digit in
///          unchanged. That loop runs only for counts below 32 times
///          (precision - 1), and it changes the result only when the
///          digits it ORs in are non-zero. In a number of four or more
///          digits, for example, shifting 1 left by 64 sets bits 64 and 96;
///          in a three-digit number the same shift is correct.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Because the faulty shift is undefined in C, a build with another
/// compiler or for another CPU may produce different results for those
/// counts. No game code is known to reach this routine (see
/// \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath, which notes the limits of that
/// evidence), so a port can correct the general path.
/// \see XMP_Shift_Right_Bits, XMP_Rotate_Left

/// \fn void XMP_Shift_Right_Bits(digit *number, int bits, int precision)
/// \brief Shifts a number right by any number of bits, in place, filling
///        with zeros.
///
/// A logical shift: zeros enter at the top, so a negative number becomes
/// positive rather than staying negative. Bits shifted out of the bottom
/// are lost. The three paths mirror XMP_Shift_Left_Bits: exactly 32 moves
/// whole digits, less than 32 ripples the low bits of each digit into the
/// one below, and a larger count does both at once.
///
/// In the source, its callers are XMP_Prepare_Modulus, which uses it to
/// scale the modulus's top two digits down to exactly 32 significant bits
/// and to halve the reciprocal, and XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test. int.h also builds
/// Int's >> and >>= operators on it.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number to shift.
/// \param bits      Shift count, 0 or more.
/// \param precision Digit count of number.
/// \warning Counts that are a multiple of 32 other than 32 itself can give
///          a wrong result, for the reason given under XMP_Shift_Left_Bits:
///          the general path shifts the digit above left by 32, which x86
///          executes as a shift by 0. As there, the faulty loop runs only
///          for counts below 32 times (precision - 1), and it changes the
///          result only when the digits it ORs in are non-zero.
///          XMP_Prepare_Modulus never asks for such a count.
///          XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test shifts by the position of the lowest set
///          bit of w - 1, which can be such a count; no retail code is known
///          to call it (see \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath).
/// \see XMP_Shift_Left_Bits

/// \fn unsigned XMP_Count_Bits(const digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Returns how many bits a number occupies: the position of its
///        highest set bit plus one.
///
/// XMP_Significance first finds the highest non-zero digit; the function
/// then scans that digit from its top bit down. The number is read as
/// unsigned, so any negative value reports the full width of precision
/// digits.
///
/// In the source, several routines here call it to skip leading zeros before
/// a bit-by-bit loop: XMP_Unsigned_Mult, XMP_Unsigned_Div,
/// XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int, XMP_Reciprocal, XMP_Exponent_Mod and
/// XMP_Randomize_Bounded among them. XMP_Prepare_Modulus uses it to measure
/// the modulus. Outside mpmath.cpp, Int::BitCount wraps it, and PKey's
/// DER-decoding constructor, PKey::Decode_Modulus and PKey::Generate use it
/// to set a key's bit precision from its modulus.
///
/// \param number    The number to measure.
/// \param precision Its digit count.
/// \return The significant bit count, from 0 for zero up to 32 times
///         precision.
/// \see XMP_Significance, XMP_Count_Bytes

/// \fn bool XMP_Rotate_Left(digit *number, bool carry, int precision)
/// \brief Shifts a number left by one bit through a carry bit.
///
/// The incoming carry becomes the new bit 0, every other bit moves up one
/// place, and the old top bit is returned as the outgoing carry. Chaining
/// calls therefore rotates a number through the carry, as a CPU's
/// rotate-through-carry instruction does.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number to rotate.
/// \param carry     The bit shifted in at the bottom.
/// \param precision Digit count of number.
/// \return The bit shifted out of the top.
/// \note A scan of the retail image finds no call, jump or stored address
///       that targets it, and no WWLib source calls it, in BFME 2 or in
///       Zero Hour.
/// \see XMP_Shift_Left_Bits

/// \fn void XMP_Neg(digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Negates a number in place, in two's complement.
///
/// Inverts every bit and adds one (XMP_Not, then XMP_Inc). Zero stays
/// zero. The most negative value, with only the top bit set, has no
/// positive counterpart and comes back unchanged.
///
/// In the source, its callers are XMP_Abs, which takes the magnitude;
/// XMP_Signed_Mult, XMP_Signed_Mult_Int and XMP_Signed_Div, which work on
/// magnitudes and fix the sign afterwards; XMP_Decode_ASCII, which applies
/// a leading minus sign; and XMP_Mod_Mult. XMP_Mod_Mult calls it twice, to
/// turn its dividend into a one's complement and to turn the remainder back
/// at the end; retail compiles both calls inline. The other five callers
/// reach the out-of-line retail copy. int.h also gives Int a Negate method
/// built on it.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number to negate.
/// \param precision Digit count of number.
/// \see XMP_Abs, XMP_Not, XMP_Inc

/// \fn void XMP_Hybrid_Mul(unsigned short *prod, unsigned short *multiplicand, unsigned short multiplier, int precision)
/// \brief Adds multiplicand times a 16-bit multiplier into prod, working in
///        16-bit units.
///
/// The inner loop of both multiplication-based routines behind
/// XMP_Mod_Mult. For each of precision units it adds multiplicand's unit
/// times multiplier, plus the running carry, to prod's unit, stores the low
/// 16 bits and carries the rest. The final carry is added to the unit just
/// above the range, prod[precision], and goes no further. A 16-bit by
/// 16-bit product plus two 16-bit addends always fits in a 32-bit
/// unsigned long, so working in 16-bit units keeps every intermediate
/// within 32 bits.
///
/// XMP_Double_Mul calls it once per multiplier unit to add each row of a
/// schoolbook product. XMP_Mod_Mult calls it to add the modulus times the
/// trial quotient from mp_quo_digit to its dividend, which it holds as a
/// one's complement so that the addition performs the subtraction.
///
/// Unlike the XMP_ routines in mpmath.h, it is declared only in
/// mpmath.cpp and has C++ linkage.
///
/// \param prod         [in,out] The accumulator: precision units, plus the
///                     unit above them that takes the final carry.
/// \param multiplicand precision units to multiply.
/// \param multiplier   The 16-bit factor.
/// \param precision    Count of 16-bit units, not digits. Both callers pass
///                     twice a digit count.
/// \see XMP_Double_Mul, XMP_Mod_Mult, mp_quo_digit

/// \fn unsigned short mp_quo_digit(unsigned short *dividend)
/// \brief Estimates one 16-bit unit of the quotient during XMP_Mod_Mult's
///        reduction.
///
/// Called only by XMP_Mod_Mult (in the source and in retail), once per
/// quotient unit, in the long division that reduces a double-width product
/// by the modulus. The source's comment calls it the heart of Smith's modulo
/// reduction.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// It reads the unit at dividend and the two units below it. Because
/// XMP_Mod_Mult keeps the dividend as its one's complement, each unit is
/// complemented back first. The three units are multiplied by the two
/// 16-bit halves of the modulus reciprocal that XMP_Prepare_Modulus
/// staged, the partial products are summed with deliberate rounding up,
/// and the sum is shifted right by the staged shift count and capped at
/// 65535. The source's comment explains the rounding: the estimate must
/// never be too small, though it may occasionally be too large, because a
/// negative remainder is easier to detect than one that still exceeds the
/// divisor. XMP_Mod_Mult corrects a too-large estimate once: it subtracts
/// the modulus from its one's-complement dividend, which adds the modulus
/// back to the true remainder.
///
/// \param dividend Points at the most significant unit of the current
///                 three-unit window; dividend[-1] and dividend[-2] are
///                 read too.
/// \return The trial quotient unit, 0 to 65535.
/// \warning It reads the reciprocal and shift count from the statics that
///          XMP_Prepare_Modulus sets, so it is meaningful only between that
///          call and XMP_Mod_Mult_Clear. It has C++ linkage and is declared
///          only in mpmath.cpp.
/// \see XMP_Mod_Mult, XMP_Prepare_Modulus, XMP_Hybrid_Mul
